/* PaKuWord – English daily pack 2026-11-23: new domain Mathematics (30) + 30 words for existing domains */
window.PW_CONTENT = window.PW_CONTENT || {};
window.PW_CONTENT.en = (window.PW_CONTENT.en || []).concat([
/* ---------- MATHEMATICS ---------- */
{id:"mat_prime",cat:"mat",t:"Prime number",
 s:"A whole number greater than one that can only be divided exactly by one and by itself.",
 l:"A prime number has exactly two divisors: one and the number itself. Two, three, five, seven and eleven are primes, while nine is not, because it can be divided by three. The number one is not counted as prime, and two is the only even prime. Every whole number bigger than one can be built by multiplying primes together in just one way, which is why primes are often called the building blocks of numbers. Today they also protect online banking and messages, because huge primes are hard to work out from their products.",
 x:"Thirteen is a prime number, so you can't share thirteen sweets equally between two, three or four friends.",
 f:"More than two thousand years ago the Greek mathematician Euclid proved that there is no largest prime: however far you count, there are always more.",
 js:"A number you can only split into equal groups of one, or one big group.",
 jl:"Try sharing seven marbles into equal groups. You can make seven groups of one, or one group of seven – nothing else works! Numbers like that are called prime numbers. Two, three, five and seven are primes. Eight is not, because you can make two groups of four.",
 jx:"Leo found out that seventeen is a prime number, because no team size except one or seventeen works."},
{id:"mat_fraction",cat:"mat",t:"Fraction",
 s:"A number that shows a part of a whole, written as one number over another.",
 l:"A fraction such as three quarters describes parts of a whole. The bottom number, the denominator, says into how many equal parts the whole is divided; the top number, the numerator, says how many of those parts we take. Fractions can also mean division: three quarters is three divided by four, which equals zero point seven five. Different-looking fractions can be equal, like one half and two quarters, and simplifying a fraction means writing it with the smallest possible numbers.",
 x:"The recipe needs a third of a cup of sugar, so I filled the cup one third of the way up.",
 f:"The word fraction comes from the Latin fractio, meaning “a breaking” – the same root as fracture and fragment.",
 js:"A part of something whole, like half a pizza.",
 jl:"When you cut a cake into four equal slices and eat one, you've eaten one quarter. We write it as a one on top and a four underneath, with a line between them. The bottom number tells how many pieces there are, and the top number tells how many you have.",
 jx:"Ava ate two of the eight slices of pizza – that's two eighths, or one quarter."},
{id:"mat_percentage",cat:"mat",t:"Percentage",
 s:"A way of expressing a part of something as a number out of one hundred.",
 l:"Per cent means “out of a hundred”, so fifty per cent is fifty out of every hundred, or one half. Percentages make it easy to compare things of different sizes: a score of eighteen out of twenty and forty-five out of fifty are both ninety per cent. They are everywhere in daily life, from discounts and interest rates to battery levels and weather forecasts. A common trap is that a rise of ten per cent followed by a fall of ten per cent does not bring you back to where you started.",
 x:"The jacket was twenty-five per cent off, so instead of eighty pounds it cost sixty.",
 f:"Per cent comes from the Latin per centum, “for each hundred”. The per cent sign developed gradually from handwritten abbreviations of the Italian per cento used by merchants.",
 js:"A number that tells how many out of one hundred.",
 jl:"Imagine one hundred sweets in a jar. If you eat fifty, you've eaten fifty per cent – that's half! One hundred per cent means all of them. Your tablet battery shows a percentage too: at one hundred per cent it's full.",
 jx:"My tablet battery is at twenty per cent, so I should charge it soon."},
{id:"mat_pi",cat:"mat",t:"Pi (π)",
 s:"The number you get when you divide the distance around any circle by the distance across it, about 3.14.",
 l:"Pi, written with the Greek letter π, is the ratio of a circle's circumference to its diameter. It is the same for every circle, from a coin to a planet's orbit. Its value starts three point one four one five nine and its digits go on forever without repeating, because pi cannot be written exactly as a fraction. Pi appears in formulas for the area of a circle, the volume of a sphere and even in physics and statistics, far away from circles.",
 x:"To find the length of the fence around the round pond, she multiplied its width by pi.",
 f:"The Welsh mathematician William Jones first used the letter π for this number in 1706, and Leonhard Euler made it popular. Many people celebrate Pi Day on the fourteenth of March, written 3/14 in the American style.",
 js:"A special number, about three point one four, that belongs to every circle.",
 jl:"Take a round plate and measure across the middle. Then measure all the way around the edge. The edge is always a bit more than three times longer! That “a bit more than three” is a special number called pi.",
 jx:"With a piece of string, Mia found that the edge of her bike wheel was about three times longer than its width."},
{id:"mat_equation",cat:"mat",t:"Equation",
 s:"A mathematical statement that two things are equal, written with an equals sign.",
 l:"An equation says that what is on the left of the equals sign has the same value as what is on the right, for example two plus three equals five. Often an equation contains an unknown, usually written as x, and solving it means finding the value that makes the statement true. Equations are like a balance: whatever you do to one side, you must do to the other to keep it level. Scientists use equations to describe how the world works, from falling objects to the growth of savings.",
 x:"If x plus four equals ten, then the equation tells us that x must be six.",
 f:"The equals sign was invented by the Welsh mathematician Robert Recorde in 1557. He chose two parallel lines because, as he wrote, no two things can be more equal.",
 js:"A number sentence that says two sides are the same.",
 jl:"An equation is like a see-saw that is perfectly level. On one side you might have three plus something, and on the other side seven. To keep it level, the something must be four! Finding the missing number is called solving the equation.",
 jx:"The puzzle said: box plus five equals nine. Sam worked out that the box was four."},
{id:"mat_variable",cat:"mat",t:"Variable",
 s:"A letter or symbol that stands for a number which can change or is not yet known.",
 l:"In algebra, a variable is a placeholder, usually a letter such as x, y or n, that stands for a number. Sometimes it is an unknown we want to find; sometimes it can take many values, like the number of tickets sold or the temperature during a day. Variables let us write rules that work for every case at once, for example that the area of a rectangle equals its length times its width. Computer programs also use variables, which are named boxes for storing values.",
 x:"In the formula for the cost of the trip, the variable n stands for the number of pupils.",
 f:"The habit of using x, y and z for unknowns owes a lot to the French philosopher and mathematician René Descartes, who used it in his work La Géométrie in 1637.",
 js:"A letter that stands for a number we don't know yet.",
 jl:"Sometimes in maths we use a letter instead of a number, like a secret code. If x stands for how many apples are in a bag, and two bags hold ten apples, then x is five. The letter is called a variable, because the number it stands for can change.",
 jx:"In our code game, the variable “score” goes up by one each time you catch a star."},
{id:"mat_function",cat:"mat",t:"Function",
 s:"A rule that gives exactly one output for every input.",
 l:"A function is like a machine: you put a number in, the rule does something to it, and one result comes out. For example, the rule “double it and add one” turns three into seven. Functions can be shown as formulas, tables or graphs, and they describe how one thing depends on another, such as the price of a taxi ride depending on the distance. The key rule is that each input gives only one output, though different inputs may give the same one.",
 x:"The temperature of the cooling tea is a function of time: the longer you wait, the colder it gets.",
 f:"The short notation f of x, written f(x), was introduced by the Swiss mathematician Leonhard Euler in the eighteenth century.",
 js:"A rule machine: put a number in, get a number out.",
 jl:"Imagine a magic box. Whatever number you drop in, it adds two and pushes the answer out. Put in three, out comes five. Put in ten, out comes twelve. A rule like that is called a function.",
 jx:"Our class's function machine doubled every number – Nina put in six and got twelve."},
{id:"mat_theorem",cat:"mat",t:"Theorem",
 s:"A mathematical statement that has been proved to be true.",
 l:"A theorem is a result that mathematicians have shown, by careful logical reasoning, must be true. Before it is proved, a statement that people believe but cannot yet prove is called a conjecture. Once proved, a theorem stays true forever and can be used to prove further results. Smaller helper results are sometimes called lemmas, and direct consequences of a theorem are called corollaries. Famous theorems often carry the names of people who proved them or first stated them.",
 x:"Our teacher showed us how the theorem about angles in a triangle follows from facts about parallel lines.",
 f:"Pierre de Fermat wrote around 1637 that he had a proof of what became known as Fermat's Last Theorem, but that the margin of his book was too small to contain it. It was finally proved by Andrew Wiles in the 1990s.",
 js:"A maths fact that someone has shown is always true.",
 jl:"In maths, it's not enough to say “I think this is true”. You have to show why, step by step, so everyone can check it. When someone has done that, the fact becomes a theorem – and it stays true forever.",
 jx:"Ella learned a theorem: the angles inside any triangle always add up to one hundred and eighty degrees."},
{id:"mat_proof",cat:"mat",t:"Proof",
 s:"A step-by-step argument showing that a mathematical statement must be true.",
 l:"A mathematical proof starts from facts already accepted and uses logical steps to reach a conclusion that cannot be doubted. Checking many examples is not enough, because a single counterexample could still exist somewhere. There are different styles of proof: a direct proof goes straight from the facts to the result, while a proof by contradiction assumes the opposite and shows that it leads to nonsense. Proofs are what make mathematics different from other sciences, where results are tested by experiments.",
 x:"She wrote a proof that the sum of two odd numbers is always even.",
 f:"Many proofs end with the letters QED, short for the Latin quod erat demonstrandum, “which was to be shown”.",
 js:"Showing step by step why something in maths is always true.",
 jl:"If you say “every even number can be split into two equal groups”, a friend might ask: are you sure? A proof is how you show you're right – not just with a few examples, but with reasons that work for every number.",
 jx:"Kai's proof showed that adding two even numbers always gives an even number."},
{id:"mat_axiom",cat:"mat",t:"Axiom",
 s:"A basic statement that is accepted as true without proof and used as a starting point.",
 l:"Every proof has to start somewhere, so mathematicians choose a small set of simple statements, called axioms, that they agree to accept. Everything else is then built on top of them. In geometry, for example, one axiom says that a straight line can be drawn between any two points. Changing the axioms can create a whole new branch of mathematics. Outside maths, the word is used for a principle that seems obviously true to the speaker.",
 x:"In the geometry lesson, we started from a few axioms and built up everything else from them.",
 f:"Euclid's fifth axiom, about parallel lines, puzzled mathematicians for centuries. In the nineteenth century they discovered that replacing it produces consistent geometries of curved spaces, called non-Euclidean geometry.",
 js:"A starting rule that everyone agrees is true.",
 jl:"When you build a tower of blocks, you need a bottom layer to start with. In maths, the bottom layer is made of axioms: simple rules that everyone agrees on, like “through two dots you can draw one straight line”. Everything else is built on top.",
 jx:"Our board game had a few axioms, like “everyone starts with three coins”, and all the other rules came from them."},
{id:"mat_pythagoras",cat:"mat",t:"Pythagorean theorem",
 s:"The rule that in a right-angled triangle, the square of the longest side equals the sum of the squares of the other two.",
 l:"In a triangle with one right angle, the longest side is called the hypotenuse. The Pythagorean theorem says that if you square the two shorter sides and add the results, you get the square of the hypotenuse, often written a squared plus b squared equals c squared. A triangle with sides three, four and five is the classic example, because nine plus sixteen is twenty-five. Builders, carpenters and map-makers use the rule to check right angles and work out distances.",
 x:"Using the Pythagorean theorem, he worked out how long the ladder had to be to reach the window.",
 f:"The rule is named after the Greek thinker Pythagoras, but a Babylonian clay tablet called Plimpton 322, more than a thousand years older, already lists sets of numbers that fit it.",
 js:"A famous rule about triangles with a square corner.",
 jl:"Some triangles have a perfectly square corner, like the corner of a book. Long ago, people discovered a clever rule for these triangles: if you know the two short sides, you can work out the long one. A triangle with sides three, four and five is the most famous example.",
 jx:"With a string knotted into three, four and five steps, the children made a perfect square corner on the playground."},
{id:"mat_probability",cat:"mat",t:"Probability",
 s:"A number from zero to one that shows how likely something is to happen.",
 l:"Probability measures chance. A probability of zero means something is impossible, one means it is certain, and one half means it is as likely to happen as not, like a fair coin landing heads. It can also be written as a percentage or as odds. Probability helps with weather forecasts, insurance, medicine and games, but it does not predict a single event: even if the chance of rain is high, it may stay dry. A common mistake is thinking that after several heads, tails is “due” – the coin has no memory.",
 x:"The probability of rolling a six with one fair dice is one in six.",
 f:"The mathematics of probability grew out of letters exchanged in 1654 between the French mathematicians Blaise Pascal and Pierre de Fermat about how to split the stakes of an unfinished game of chance.",
 js:"How likely it is that something will happen.",
 jl:"If you put one red and one blue ball in a bag and pick one without looking, you have a one in two chance of getting red. If all the balls are red, you're sure to get red. Probability is a way of measuring how likely something is.",
 jx:"Ben said the probability of picking his name from the hat was one in twenty-five, because there are twenty-five of us."},
{id:"mat_mean_median",cat:"mat",t:"Mean, median and mode",
 s:"Three different kinds of average: the sum divided by the count, the middle value, and the most common value.",
 l:"The mean is what most people call the average: add all the values and divide by how many there are. The median is the middle value when everything is lined up in order, and the mode is the value that appears most often. They can tell quite different stories. If a few values are extremely large, the mean is pulled upwards, while the median hardly moves, which is why incomes and house prices are often described with the median. Choosing the right average matters when reading news and statistics.",
 x:"The median height in our class was one hundred and forty centimetres, even though the mean was a little higher.",
 f:"Imagine a café with ten customers. If a billionaire walks in, the mean wealth in the room shoots up enormously, but the median barely changes – a simple way to see why the two can differ.",
 js:"Different ways of finding the “middle” or “usual” number.",
 jl:"If five friends have two, three, three, four and eight stickers, the middle number in the line is three – that's the median. The number that appears most is also three – that's the mode. If you share all the stickers out equally, everyone gets four – that's the mean.",
 jx:"The most common shoe size in our class – the mode – was thirty-four."},
{id:"mat_infinity",cat:"mat",t:"Infinity",
 s:"The idea of something without any end or limit, written with the symbol ∞.",
 l:"Infinity is not an ordinary number but the idea of endlessness. The counting numbers go on forever, because you can always add one more. Mathematicians have learned to work with infinity carefully, since it behaves strangely: there are as many even numbers as whole numbers, even though the even numbers seem to be only half of them. In the nineteenth century, Georg Cantor showed that some infinities are bigger than others, a result that shocked many of his colleagues.",
 x:"The road on the poster seemed to stretch towards infinity.",
 f:"The sideways-eight symbol for infinity was introduced by the English mathematician John Wallis in 1655.",
 js:"Something that goes on forever and never ends.",
 jl:"Think of the biggest number you know. Now add one – it's even bigger! You can do that forever, because numbers never run out. That never-ending idea is called infinity, and its sign looks like an eight lying down.",
 jx:"Lily said she loved her grandma to infinity and back."},
{id:"mat_zero",cat:"mat",t:"Zero",
 s:"The number that stands for nothing, and also a digit that holds a place in larger numbers.",
 l:"Zero has two jobs. As a number, it means none: zero apples is no apples at all. As a digit, it marks an empty place, so that we can tell five, fifty and five hundred apart. Many ancient peoples counted without it, and the idea of zero as a real number with its own rules developed in India. Strange things happen with zero: anything multiplied by it gives zero, and dividing by zero is not allowed, because no answer makes sense.",
 x:"The lift had a button marked zero for the ground floor.",
 f:"In the seventh century the Indian mathematician Brahmagupta wrote down rules for calculating with zero. The word itself travelled from the Arabic sifr, meaning “empty”, through Latin and Italian; cipher comes from the same root.",
 js:"The number that means nothing at all.",
 jl:"If you have three biscuits and eat all three, how many are left? Zero! Zero also helps write big numbers: in ten, the zero shows there are no single ones. Without zero, it would be hard to tell one from ten or one hundred.",
 jx:"At the end of the game, the score was three to zero."},
{id:"mat_symmetry",cat:"mat",t:"Symmetry",
 s:"The property of a shape that looks the same after it is flipped, turned or moved.",
 l:"A shape has mirror symmetry, or line symmetry, if one half is the reflection of the other, like a butterfly. It has rotational symmetry if it looks the same after being turned part of the way round, like a star or a windmill. Symmetry appears in nature, art, architecture and physics, where it helps scientists find deep laws. People often find symmetrical faces and buildings pleasing, although a little asymmetry can make things more interesting.",
 x:"The letter A has a line of symmetry running down its middle.",
 f:"Snowflakes almost always have six arms, because the water molecules in ice crystals fit together in a hexagonal pattern.",
 js:"When both halves of something match, like a mirror picture.",
 jl:"Fold a paper heart down the middle: both halves fit perfectly on top of each other. That's symmetry! Butterflies, many leaves and your own face are almost symmetrical. Some shapes, like stars, look the same when you turn them round.",
 jx:"Jada painted one side of the paper, folded it, and got a symmetrical butterfly."},
{id:"mat_algorithm",cat:"mat",t:"Algorithm",
 s:"A precise, step-by-step set of instructions for solving a problem or doing a task.",
 l:"An algorithm is a recipe that can be followed exactly, without any guessing. Long division, sorting books by title and finding the shortest route on a map can all be done with algorithms. A good algorithm always finishes, gives the right result and does not waste time. Computers are machines for following algorithms very quickly, which is why the word is now linked to search engines and social media feeds that decide what we see.",
 x:"The app uses an algorithm to find the quickest bus connection.",
 f:"The word comes from the name of the ninth-century Persian scholar Al-Khwarizmi, whose book on calculating with Hindu-Arabic numerals was known in Latin under his name, Algoritmi.",
 js:"A list of steps you follow in the right order to get something done.",
 jl:"Brushing your teeth has steps: put toothpaste on the brush, brush the top teeth, brush the bottom teeth, rinse. If you follow clear steps in order and always get the job done, that's an algorithm. Computers follow algorithms all day long.",
 jx:"Our robot game used an algorithm: two steps forward, turn left, one step forward."},
{id:"mat_exponent",cat:"mat",t:"Exponent (power)",
 s:"A small raised number that shows how many times a number is multiplied by itself.",
 l:"In two to the power of three, written as a small three above the two, the three is the exponent. It means two times two times two, which is eight. Exponents are a short way of writing very big and very small numbers: ten to the power of six is a million. Things that grow by repeated multiplication, such as savings with interest or bacteria dividing, show exponential growth, which starts slowly and then becomes astonishingly fast.",
 x:"Five squared, or five to the power of two, equals twenty-five.",
 f:"The number ten to the power of one hundred is called a googol. The name was suggested by the young nephew of the American mathematician Edward Kasner.",
 js:"A tiny number that tells you how many times to multiply.",
 jl:"Two times two times two is a lot to write. So we write a big two with a tiny three up in the corner. The tiny number is the exponent, and it means “multiply three twos together”. The answer is eight.",
 jx:"Ten to the power of two is ten times ten, which is one hundred."},
{id:"mat_square_root",cat:"mat",t:"Square root",
 s:"The number that, multiplied by itself, gives a chosen number.",
 l:"The square root of nine is three, because three times three is nine. It is the reverse of squaring a number. The name comes from geometry: a square with an area of sixteen has sides four units long. Some square roots are whole numbers, but most are not; the square root of two, for example, has decimals that never end or repeat. Square roots are used to work out distances, in the Pythagorean theorem, and in statistics.",
 x:"The square room has an area of twenty-five square metres, so each wall is the square root of twenty-five, five metres long.",
 f:"The word root translates the Latin radix. The tick-shaped sign for square roots appeared in print in a German algebra book by Christoff Rudolff in 1525.",
 js:"The number that times itself makes another number.",
 jl:"Four times four is sixteen. So we say four is the square root of sixteen. You can picture it with tiles: sixteen tiles make a square that is four tiles long on each side.",
 jx:"Theo arranged forty-nine tiles into a square with seven on each side – seven is the square root of forty-nine."},
{id:"mat_factorial",cat:"mat",t:"Factorial",
 s:"The result of multiplying a whole number by every smaller whole number down to one.",
 l:"Five factorial, written five with an exclamation mark, means five times four times three times two times one, which is one hundred and twenty. Factorials tell us how many different ways there are to arrange things in order: three books can stand on a shelf in six different ways. They grow incredibly fast, which is why the number of possible orders of a deck of cards, or of a class lining up, is so enormous. By agreement, zero factorial equals one.",
 x:"Four friends can line up for a photo in four factorial, that is twenty-four, different ways.",
 f:"A deck of fifty-two cards can be arranged in about eight followed by sixty-seven zeros different ways, so a well-shuffled deck is almost certainly in an order no one has ever seen before. The exclamation mark notation was introduced by Christian Kramp in 1808.",
 js:"Multiplying a number by all the smaller numbers down to one.",
 jl:"How many ways can three teddies sit in a row? Try it: there are six! You get six by doing three times two times one. That kind of countdown multiplying is called a factorial, and it is written with an exclamation mark.",
 jx:"Maya counted that her three toy cars could be parked in six different orders."},
{id:"mat_fibonacci",cat:"mat",t:"Fibonacci sequence",
 s:"A list of numbers in which each number is the sum of the two before it: 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13…",
 l:"The Fibonacci sequence starts with one and one, and each new number is made by adding the previous two: two, three, five, eight, thirteen, twenty-one and so on. It appears surprisingly often in nature, for example in the number of spirals in sunflower heads and pine cones. The sequence is also linked to the golden ratio: dividing a Fibonacci number by the one before it gets closer and closer to about one point six one eight. Programmers often use it as a first exercise in writing loops.",
 x:"The spirals on the pine cone came in eight and thirteen – two neighbouring Fibonacci numbers.",
 f:"Leonardo of Pisa, later nicknamed Fibonacci, described the sequence in his book Liber Abaci in 1202 as a puzzle about breeding rabbits. The same book helped spread Hindu-Arabic numerals in Europe.",
 js:"A number pattern where you add the last two numbers to get the next.",
 jl:"Start with one and one. Add them: two. Add the last two, one and two: three. Then two and three: five. Keep going: eight, thirteen, twenty-one! This is the Fibonacci pattern, and you can find it in sunflowers and pine cones.",
 jx:"Zara counted the petals on a flower and got five – a Fibonacci number!"},
{id:"mat_golden_ratio",cat:"mat",t:"Golden ratio",
 s:"A special proportion, about 1.618, in which the whole relates to the larger part as the larger part relates to the smaller.",
 l:"Divide a line into two pieces so that the whole line compared with the longer piece is the same as the longer piece compared with the shorter one. The ratio you get is the golden ratio, usually written with the Greek letter phi, roughly one point six one eight. It appears in geometry, for example in the regular pentagon and five-pointed star, and it is closely tied to the Fibonacci sequence. Artists and designers have used it on purpose, especially since the Renaissance.",
 x:"The architect chose the golden ratio for the proportions of the windows.",
 f:"Many popular claims that the Parthenon, the Mona Lisa or the human body are built on the golden ratio are exaggerated or rest on hand-picked measurements.",
 js:"A special way of dividing something that many people find beautiful.",
 jl:"Some rectangles look especially nice to many people – not too long, not too square. Their sides follow a special number, a bit more than one and a half. It's called the golden ratio, and you can find it in five-pointed stars too.",
 jx:"Our art teacher showed us how to draw a golden rectangle with a ruler."},
{id:"mat_set",cat:"mat",t:"Set",
 s:"A collection of distinct objects, called elements, considered as a whole.",
 l:"In mathematics, a set is simply a group of things, such as the set of even numbers or the set of pupils in a class. Each element appears only once and the order does not matter. Sets can be combined: their union contains everything in either set, and their intersection contains only what they share. A set with nothing in it is called the empty set. Set theory became the language in which much of modern mathematics is written.",
 x:"The set of primary colours in painting is often given as red, yellow and blue.",
 f:"Set theory was founded by the German mathematician Georg Cantor in the 1870s. The overlapping circles used to picture sets are called Venn diagrams, after the English logician John Venn, who described them in 1880.",
 js:"A group of things that belong together.",
 jl:"Put all your red toys in one box – that's the set of red toys. Put all your cars in another – that's the set of cars. A red car belongs to both sets! Maths sets are just groups of things, and drawing circles helps us see them.",
 jx:"In our Venn diagram, the cat belonged to the set “pets” and to the set “has fur”."},
{id:"mat_vector",cat:"mat",t:"Vector",
 s:"A quantity that has both a size and a direction, often drawn as an arrow.",
 l:"Some quantities need only a number, like temperature or mass. Others also need a direction: a wind of twenty kilometres an hour blowing north is different from one blowing south. These are vectors, and they are drawn as arrows whose length shows the size and whose tip shows the direction. Vectors can be added by placing arrows one after another. They are essential in physics, navigation, computer games and graphics, where so-called vector images can be enlarged without becoming blurry.",
 x:"The pilot added the wind's vector to the plane's own to find its true path.",
 f:"Vector comes from the Latin vehere, “to carry”. In biology the same word means an animal, such as a mosquito, that carries a disease from one host to another.",
 js:"An arrow that shows how far and which way.",
 jl:"If a treasure map says “walk five steps towards the big tree”, it tells you two things: how far and which way. In maths, we draw that as an arrow and call it a vector. Long arrows mean far, short arrows mean near.",
 jx:"In the treasure hunt, each clue was a vector: three steps east, then two steps north."},
{id:"mat_logarithm",cat:"mat",t:"Logarithm",
 s:"The power to which a base number must be raised to get a given number.",
 l:"A logarithm answers the question: how many times must I multiply the base by itself to get this number? With base ten, the logarithm of one thousand is three, because ten times ten times ten is one thousand. Before calculators, logarithms turned difficult multiplication into easier addition, using printed tables and slide rules. Today they are used for scales that cover huge ranges, such as decibels for loudness and the pH scale for acidity.",
 x:"Because the sound scale is based on logarithms, a small change in decibels means a big change in sound energy.",
 f:"The Scottish mathematician John Napier published the first tables of logarithms in 1614, and they quickly saved astronomers and sailors enormous amounts of calculation.",
 js:"A way of counting how many times you multiply to reach a number.",
 jl:"How many times do you need to multiply ten by itself to get one thousand? Ten, one hundred, one thousand – three times! The answer, three, is called a logarithm. Long ago, logarithms helped sailors and scientists do hard sums before calculators existed.",
 jx:"Grandpa showed us his old slide rule, which uses logarithms to multiply."},
{id:"mat_correlation",cat:"mat",t:"Correlation",
 s:"A relationship in which two things tend to change together.",
 l:"Two quantities are correlated when one tends to go up or down along with the other. Height and shoe size are positively correlated, while the temperature outside and heating bills tend to move in opposite directions, a negative correlation. Correlation is measured with a number from minus one to plus one. The most important lesson is that correlation does not prove causation: two things may move together by chance, or because a third factor affects both.",
 x:"Ice cream sales and swimming accidents are correlated, but the real cause of both is hot weather.",
 f:"The idea of correlation was developed by the English scientist Francis Galton in the 1880s, and Karl Pearson later gave it the formula still used today.",
 js:"When two things often change together.",
 jl:"On hot days, more people buy ice cream, and more people go to the beach. The two things go up together – that's called correlation. But buying ice cream doesn't make people go to the beach! Both happen because it's hot.",
 jx:"Our class found a correlation: children who read more at home also borrowed more library books."},
{id:"mat_en_bodmas",cat:"mat",only:1,t:"BIDMAS (BODMAS)",
 s:"A memory aid used in British schools for the order in which to do operations in a calculation.",
 l:"BIDMAS stands for Brackets, Indices, Division, Multiplication, Addition and Subtraction; BODMAS uses Orders or “Of” instead of Indices. It reminds pupils to work out brackets first, then powers, then division and multiplication, and finally addition and subtraction. Division and multiplication have equal rank and are done from left to right, and the same goes for addition and subtraction – a detail the acronym can hide. Without an agreed order, three plus four times two could mean either fourteen or eleven; the correct answer is eleven.",
 x:"Remember BIDMAS: do the brackets before you multiply.",
 f:"In the United States the same rule is usually taught as PEMDAS, remembered with the phrase “Please Excuse My Dear Aunt Sally”.",
 js:"A word that helps you remember which part of a sum to do first.",
 jl:"In a sum like two plus three times four, what do you do first? BIDMAS helps: B is for brackets, I for indices or powers, D and M for dividing and multiplying, A and S for adding and subtracting. So you multiply first, then add, and get fourteen.",
 jx:"Ruby used BIDMAS and got the right answer when the rest of the table got it wrong."},
{id:"mat_en_maths",cat:"mat",only:1,t:"Maths / math",
 s:"The short everyday name for mathematics: maths in British English, math in American English.",
 l:"Both forms are shortenings of mathematics. In the United Kingdom, Ireland, Australia and many other countries, people say maths, keeping the final s of the full word. In the United States and Canada, the usual form is math. Each side sometimes finds the other strange, but both are correct in their own variety of English. The full word is treated as singular in both: we say “maths is fun”, not “maths are fun”.",
 x:"In London she had maths on Monday mornings; when she moved to Boston, it became math class.",
 f:"Mathematics comes from the Greek mathēma, meaning “that which is learned” or simply “lesson”.",
 js:"The short word for mathematics – maths in Britain, math in America.",
 jl:"Mathematics is a long word, so people shorten it. In Britain we say maths, with an s at the end. In America they say math, without it. Both mean exactly the same lessons with numbers and shapes.",
 jx:"My American pen pal wrote that math is her favourite subject – just like maths is mine."},
{id:"mat_en_imperial",cat:"mat",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"Imperial units",
 s:"The traditional British system of measurement, with units such as inches, feet, miles, pints and stones.",
 l:"Imperial units were standardised in Britain in 1824 and once used across the British Empire. Today the United Kingdom mostly uses the metric system, but imperial units survive in everyday life: road signs show distances in miles and speeds in miles per hour, beer and milk can be sold in pints, and many people still give their height in feet and inches. The American system is similar but not identical; for example, an imperial pint is larger than a US pint.",
 x:"The road sign said the next town was twelve miles away.",
 f:"Since an international agreement in 1959, an inch has been defined as exactly two point five four centimetres.",
 js:"Old British ways of measuring, like miles, feet and pints.",
 jl:"In school we measure in centimetres and metres. But in Britain you'll also hear older measures: miles on road signs, pints of milk, and feet and inches for how tall you are. These are called imperial units.",
 jx:"Grandad said I'm four feet tall now, and Mum said that's about one hundred and twenty centimetres."},
{id:"mat_en_bakers_dozen",cat:"mat",only:1,t:"Baker's dozen",
 s:"Thirteen, one more than an ordinary dozen.",
 l:"A dozen means twelve, a handy number because it can be divided by two, three, four and six. A baker's dozen means thirteen. The phrase is used for real when someone gets a bonus item, and playfully when a list or a set has one extra. The dozen itself remains common in English shops, where eggs and doughnuts are still sold by the half dozen or dozen, long after decimal money arrived.",
 x:"I ordered twelve rolls and the shop gave me a baker's dozen.",
 f:"The most common explanation is that medieval English bakers added an extra loaf to avoid being punished for selling short weight under strict bread laws.",
 js:"Thirteen – a dozen plus one extra.",
 jl:"A dozen is twelve, like twelve eggs in a box. A baker's dozen is thirteen. People say long ago bakers gave one extra bun so nobody could say they were cheated.",
 jx:"Gran baked a baker's dozen of cookies, so there was one extra for me!"},
/* ---------- MEDICINE ---------- */
{id:"m_antibiotic_resistance",cat:"med",t:"Antibiotic resistance",
 s:"The ability of bacteria to survive medicines that used to kill them.",
 l:"Antibiotics are medicines that kill bacteria or stop them from multiplying. Every time they are used, a few bacteria may survive because of small differences in their genes, and these survivors pass the trait on. Over time, whole strains can become resistant, making infections harder to treat. Using antibiotics when they are not needed, for example for colds caused by viruses, or not taking them as prescribed, speeds this up. Antibiotics do not work against viruses at all.",
 x:"The doctor explained that she would not prescribe antibiotics for a cold, partly because of antibiotic resistance.",
 f:"Alexander Fleming, who discovered penicillin, warned in his Nobel Prize lecture in 1945 that misusing it could make bacteria resistant.",
 js:"When germs learn to survive the medicine that should beat them.",
 jl:"Some illnesses are caused by tiny living things called bacteria. Medicines called antibiotics can fight them. But if we use these medicines too often, some bacteria get tough and the medicine stops working on them. That's why doctors only give antibiotics when they're really needed.",
 jx:"The doctor said Oscar's cold was a virus, so antibiotics wouldn't help – rest and drinks would."},
{id:"m_blood_pressure",cat:"med",t:"Blood pressure",
 s:"The force with which blood pushes against the walls of the arteries.",
 l:"Each time the heart beats, it pushes blood into the arteries, and the pressure rises; between beats it falls. Blood pressure is therefore given as two numbers: the higher systolic pressure during a heartbeat and the lower diastolic pressure between beats. It is measured with a cuff around the arm. Pressure that stays too high for a long time often causes no symptoms but strains the heart, brain and kidneys, which is why regular checks are recommended for adults.",
 x:"The nurse wrapped the cuff around his arm to measure his blood pressure.",
 f:"Blood pressure is still given in millimetres of mercury, because early devices measured how high it could push a column of mercury. The English clergyman Stephen Hales made one of the first measurements, on a horse, and described it in 1733.",
 js:"How hard your blood pushes as your heart pumps it around your body.",
 jl:"Your heart is a pump that sends blood through tubes all over your body. The blood presses on the walls of these tubes, a bit like water in a garden hose. Doctors measure this push with a soft band that squeezes your arm. It's called blood pressure.",
 jx:"At the check-up, the band on Grandma's arm squeezed tight to measure her blood pressure."},
{id:"m_benign_malignant",cat:"med",j:false,t:"Benign vs malignant",
 s:"Benign describes a growth that does not spread; malignant describes one that can invade and spread.",
 l:"A tumour is a lump of cells that grow more than they should. If it is benign, it stays in one place, does not invade nearby tissue and does not spread to other parts of the body, though it may still need treatment if it presses on something. A malignant tumour is a cancer: its cells can grow into surrounding tissue and travel through the blood or lymph to form new tumours. Doctors usually decide which it is by examining a small sample under a microscope.",
 x:"She was relieved when the results showed the lump was benign.",
 f:"Benign comes from the Latin benignus, “kind”, and malignant from malignus, “ill-willed”. The word cancer is Latin for “crab”, a name linked to ancient Greek physicians who compared some tumours to a crab's shape."},
{id:"m_allergy",cat:"med",t:"Allergy",
 s:"An overreaction of the immune system to something that is normally harmless.",
 l:"In an allergy, the body's defence system mistakes a harmless substance, such as pollen, dust mites, pet hair or certain foods, for a danger. It then releases chemicals like histamine, causing sneezing, itching, rashes, a runny nose or swelling. Most reactions are mild, but a severe, whole-body reaction called anaphylaxis is a medical emergency. Allergies are different from food intolerances, which do not involve the immune system in the same way.",
 x:"He sneezes every spring because of his allergy to birch pollen.",
 f:"The word allergy was coined in 1906 by the Austrian paediatrician Clemens von Pirquet, from Greek words meaning “other” and “work” – the body reacting in an altered way.",
 js:"When your body thinks something harmless is dangerous and reacts to it.",
 jl:"Your body has a guard team that fights germs. Sometimes the guards get confused and attack things that are harmless, like flower dust or cat hair. Then you might sneeze, itch or get a rash. That's an allergy. Some people with allergies need to avoid certain foods.",
 jx:"Sofia has an allergy to peanuts, so her friends check the snacks before sharing."},
{id:"m_side_effect",cat:"med",t:"Side effect",
 s:"An unintended effect of a medicine or treatment, in addition to the one it is meant to have.",
 l:"Medicines are designed to do a particular job, but they can also affect other parts of the body. These extra effects are side effects. Some are common and mild, like drowsiness or an upset stomach, while others are rare but serious. The leaflet in the box lists effects that people reported during studies and use, which does not mean everyone will get them. Anyone worried about a side effect should talk to a doctor or pharmacist rather than simply stop treatment.",
 x:"One side effect of the hay fever tablets was that they made him a little sleepy.",
 f:"Simply reading about possible side effects can make some people notice them more – the so-called nocebo effect, the opposite of the placebo effect.",
 js:"Something extra a medicine does that it wasn't meant to do.",
 jl:"A medicine is made to help with one thing, like a headache. But sometimes it also does something else – maybe it makes you sleepy or gives you a funny tummy. That extra thing is called a side effect. Grown-ups and doctors keep an eye on it.",
 jx:"Mum said the medicine might make me sleepy – that's a side effect."},
{id:"m_en_gp",cat:"med",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"GP (general practitioner)",
 s:"A family doctor in the UK, usually the first person you see about a health problem.",
 l:"In the United Kingdom, people register with a GP surgery near where they live. GPs deal with a wide range of everyday health problems, give vaccinations and long-term care, and refer patients to hospital specialists when needed, so they act as the gateway to most of the National Health Service. Appointments may be face to face, by phone or online. In an emergency people go to accident and emergency instead, and for urgent advice they can call the NHS line one one one.",
 x:"I've booked an appointment with my GP about the cough that won't go away.",
 f:"The National Health Service was launched on the fifth of July 1948, offering care free at the point of use.",
 js:"The family doctor you see first when you feel unwell in the UK.",
 jl:"When you're poorly and it's not an emergency, you usually go to your GP. That's a doctor at a local surgery who knows about lots of different health problems. If you need a special doctor, your GP will send you to one.",
 jx:"Dad took me to the GP when my ear hurt for three days."},
/* ---------- LAW ---------- */
{id:"l_will",cat:"law",c:"England & Wales",j:false,t:"Will",
 s:"A legal document saying who should receive a person's property after their death.",
 l:"In England and Wales, a will is valid if it is in writing and signed by the person making it in the presence of two witnesses, who are present at the same time and also sign. The person must be at least eighteen and understand what they are doing. A will usually names executors, who deal with the estate, and can appoint guardians for children. If someone dies without a valid will, the rules of intestacy decide who inherits. Marriage generally cancels an earlier will.",
 x:"She updated her will after her grandchildren were born.",
 f:"Under the Wills Act 1837, a witness who is also left something in the will, or whose spouse is, generally loses that gift, although the will itself stays valid."},
{id:"l_age_majority",cat:"law",c:"England & Wales",t:"Age of majority",
 s:"The age at which a person is legally treated as an adult.",
 l:"In England and Wales, a person becomes an adult in law at eighteen. From then on they can vote in general elections, sign most contracts on their own, make a will, serve on a jury and buy alcohol. Some rights start earlier: for example, in England young people must stay in some form of education or training until eighteen, although they can leave school at sixteen. Since 2023, nobody under eighteen can marry or form a civil partnership, even with their parents' consent.",
 x:"Once he reached the age of majority, he could open a credit card in his own name.",
 f:"The age of majority in England was lowered from twenty-one to eighteen by the Family Law Reform Act 1969.",
 js:"The birthday when the law says you are a grown-up.",
 jl:"When you turn eighteen, the law says you are an adult. You can vote, sign important papers and make lots of decisions by yourself. Before that, grown-ups who look after you help make those choices.",
 jx:"My big sister is eighteen now, so she voted for the first time."},
{id:"l_defamation",cat:"law",c:"England & Wales",t:"Defamation",
 s:"Publishing a false statement that seriously harms someone's reputation.",
 l:"In England and Wales, defamation is a civil wrong, not a crime. Libel is defamation in a lasting form, such as print, broadcasts or online posts, while slander is spoken. Since the Defamation Act 2013, a claimant must show that the statement caused, or is likely to cause, serious harm to their reputation; for a business that means serious financial loss. Defences include truth, honest opinion and publication on a matter of public interest.",
 x:"The newspaper settled the defamation claim and published an apology.",
 f:"England was once known as a favoured place for “libel tourism”, where foreign claimants sued; the 2013 Act made it harder to bring such cases.",
 js:"Saying untrue things about someone that hurt their good name.",
 jl:"If someone spreads lies about you – maybe writes online that you stole something when you didn't – people might stop trusting you. Hurting someone's good name with lies is called defamation, and a court can make the person put it right.",
 jx:"Spreading a rumour that isn't true can really hurt someone – grown-ups call it defamation."},
{id:"l_gdpr",cat:"law",c:"United Kingdom",t:"GDPR (data protection)",
 s:"The rules protecting personal information about people and how organisations may use it.",
 l:"The General Data Protection Regulation started applying across the European Union in 2018. After Brexit, the United Kingdom kept its own version, the UK GDPR, alongside the Data Protection Act 2018. Organisations must have a lawful reason to use personal data, keep it secure and use no more than they need. People have rights to see the data held about them, correct it and, in some cases, have it deleted. The Information Commissioner's Office enforces these rules.",
 x:"Under GDPR, she asked the company for a copy of all the data it held about her.",
 f:"Asking an organisation for your own data is called a subject access request, and it is usually free.",
 js:"Rules that protect information about you, like your name and address.",
 jl:"Your name, address, photos and school records are information about you. Companies and schools have to look after this information carefully and not share it without a good reason. The rules that protect it are called GDPR.",
 jx:"The school asked parents before putting class photos on its website, because of data protection rules."},
{id:"l_withdrawal",cat:"law",c:"United Kingdom",t:"Cancellation right (online shopping)",
 s:"A consumer's right to change their mind and cancel most purchases made online or by phone.",
 l:"Under the Consumer Contracts Regulations 2013, people who buy goods online, by phone or by post can usually cancel within fourteen days after receiving the goods, without giving a reason. The seller must refund the price, including standard delivery, generally within fourteen days. There are exceptions, for example items made to order, perishable food and sealed goods unwrapped for hygiene reasons. This right is separate from the right to reject faulty goods.",
 x:"The shoes didn't fit, so she used her cancellation right and sent them back.",
 f:"The right exists because when shopping at a distance you cannot see or try the product before buying it, unlike in a shop.",
 js:"The right to send back things your family bought online.",
 jl:"When grown-ups buy something online, they can't touch it first. So the law gives them some days to change their mind and send most things back for their money. That's a cancellation right.",
 jx:"The jumper was too small, so Mum sent it back and got her money returned."},
{id:"l_en_small_claims",cat:"law",only:1,c:"England & Wales",t:"Small claims track",
 s:"A simpler, cheaper route in the county court for claims of lower value.",
 l:"In England and Wales, most civil claims worth up to ten thousand pounds are dealt with on the small claims track. It is designed for people without lawyers: hearings are less formal, often held in an ordinary room, and the losing side normally does not have to pay the winner's legal costs. Typical cases involve unpaid bills, faulty goods or deposits that were not returned. Many money claims can be started online.",
 x:"When the builder refused to refund her deposit, she took him to the small claims court.",
 f:"Judges on the small claims track do not wear wigs or gowns, which helps make the hearing less intimidating.",
 js:"A simple kind of court for small arguments about money.",
 jl:"If someone owes you money and won't pay, there's a special, simpler court for it. You can explain your side without a lawyer, and a judge listens and decides. It's called the small claims track.",
 jx:"Uncle Joe went to the small claims court to get back money for a broken washing machine."},
/* ---------- EVERYDAY LANGUAGE ---------- */
{id:"g_schadenfreude",cat:"gen",t:"Schadenfreude",
 s:"Pleasure felt at someone else's misfortune.",
 l:"Schadenfreude is a word borrowed from German for the little spark of satisfaction some people feel when something goes wrong for someone else, especially for a rival or someone who seemed too proud. It is a common human feeling, usually mild and fleeting, but it is not seen as kind, so people rarely admit it. English took the word because it had no single native term for it. It differs from cruelty, because it usually involves enjoying bad luck rather than causing it.",
 x:"When the boastful team lost on penalties, a few fans couldn't hide their schadenfreude.",
 f:"In German, Schaden means “harm” or “damage” and Freude means “joy”.",
 js:"Feeling a little bit happy when something goes wrong for someone else.",
 jl:"Sometimes, if a show-off trips or loses a game, a tiny part of us feels pleased. That feeling has a German name: schadenfreude. Most people feel it sometimes, but it's kinder to say “Are you okay?” instead of laughing.",
 jx:"When the boy who always teased others fell in the puddle, Mia felt a bit of schadenfreude – then helped him up."},
{id:"g_metaphor",cat:"gen",t:"Metaphor",
 s:"A figure of speech that describes something by saying it is something else.",
 l:"A metaphor connects two different things to show what they have in common, without using “like” or “as”. Saying “time is money” or “she has a heart of gold” does not mean these things literally. A simile makes the comparison openly, for example “as brave as a lion”. Everyday language is full of metaphors we hardly notice, such as the leg of a table or a bright idea. For some people, including many autistic people, literal language may be clearer, so it helps to explain metaphors when needed.",
 x:"The poet used a metaphor when she called the city a sleeping giant.",
 f:"Metaphor comes from the Greek metapherein, “to carry across” – it carries meaning from one thing to another. In modern Greek, the related word metaforá is used for transport, so you can see it on removal vans.",
 js:"Saying something is something else to make a picture in your mind.",
 jl:"If someone says “my brother is a cheeky monkey”, they don't mean he's really a monkey! They mean he's playful and silly, like a monkey. Using one thing to describe another like this is called a metaphor.",
 jx:"Our teacher said, “This classroom is a zoo!” – that's a metaphor for being noisy."},
{id:"g_sarcasm",cat:"gen",t:"Sarcasm",
 s:"Saying the opposite of what you mean, often to mock or show annoyance.",
 l:"Sarcasm uses words that sound positive to express something negative, such as saying “Oh, great!” when the bus drives away. Listeners usually recognise it from tone of voice, facial expressions or the situation. It can be funny between friends but hurtful when aimed at someone. Sarcasm is a type of verbal irony, but irony does not have to be mocking. Because the clues are so subtle, sarcasm is easy to miss in text messages and hard for some people to read, so being direct is often kinder.",
 x:"“Wonderful weather for a picnic,” he said sarcastically, as rain poured down.",
 f:"The word comes from the Greek sarkazein, which originally meant “to tear flesh”.",
 js:"Saying the opposite of what you mean, often in a teasing voice.",
 jl:"If it's pouring with rain and someone says “What lovely weather!” in a funny voice, they don't really think it's lovely. They're being sarcastic – saying the opposite of what they mean. It can be confusing, so it's fine to ask “Do you really mean that?”",
 jx:"When Leo dropped his ice cream, his brother said “Nice one!” – but he was being sarcastic."},
{id:"g_cliche",cat:"gen",t:"Cliché",
 s:"A phrase or idea used so often that it has lost its originality.",
 l:"A cliché was once fresh and vivid, but repetition has worn it out. Examples include “at the end of the day”, “avoid it like the plague” and stories in which the hero wakes up and it was all a dream. Clichés are not always bad: they are quick to understand and can be comforting. In writing, though, they can make a text feel lazy, so editors often suggest replacing them with something more specific and personal.",
 x:"His speech was full of clichés, like “thinking outside the box”.",
 f:"Cliché is a French printing term. It was the name for a metal plate cast from type, used to print the same words or picture again and again.",
 js:"Words people use so often that they sound boring.",
 jl:"The first person who said “as cold as ice” had a good idea. But after millions of people said it, it became a bit boring. Phrases like that are called clichés. Writers try to find their own, new ways of saying things.",
 jx:"Instead of the cliché “it was a dark and stormy night”, Ella wrote “the wind rattled the windows like a drummer”."},
{id:"g_palindrome",cat:"gen",t:"Palindrome",
 s:"A word, number or phrase that reads the same backwards as forwards.",
 l:"Words such as level, radar and kayak are palindromes, and so is the number twelve thousand three hundred and twenty-one. Longer palindrome sentences ignore spaces and punctuation, like the famous “A man, a plan, a canal: Panama”. Writing good ones is a popular word game. Palindromes also appear in dates, music and even in DNA, where some sequences read the same on both strands in opposite directions.",
 x:"“Was it a car or a cat I saw?” is a palindrome if you ignore the spaces.",
 f:"The word comes from Greek palin, “again” or “back”, and dromos, “running” – a word that runs back again.",
 js:"A word that is the same forwards and backwards.",
 jl:"Read the word “mum” from the end: m-u-m. It's the same! Words like that are called palindromes. Other examples are “dad”, “wow” and “racecar”. Can you think of one?",
 jx:"Hannah was thrilled when she noticed her name is a palindrome."},
{id:"g_en_sorry",cat:"gen",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"Sorry (the British use)",
 s:"A word British people use not only to apologise but also to be polite, get attention or ask someone to repeat something.",
 l:"In British English, sorry does many jobs. It can be a real apology, but also a polite way to interrupt (“Sorry, is this seat taken?”), to ask someone to repeat something (“Sorry?”), or even to show mild disagreement (“Sorry, but I don't think that's right”). People sometimes say it when someone else bumps into them. Visitors may find this confusing, but it is mainly a way of keeping conversations smooth and polite.",
 x:"“Sorry, could you pass the salt?” she asked.",
 f:"Despite the similar look, sorry and sorrow are not related. Sorry comes from the Old English sārig, linked to sore, while sorrow comes from a different word, sorg.",
 js:"A word people in Britain say a lot, not only when they did something wrong.",
 jl:"In Britain, people say “sorry” all the time! Sometimes it means “I apologise”. But it can also mean “excuse me” or “what did you say?”. If someone says “Sorry?”, they just didn't hear you.",
 jx:"When Jack didn't hear the question, he said “Sorry?” and the teacher repeated it."},
/* ---------- PSYCHOLOGY ---------- */
{id:"p_growth_mindset",cat:"psy",t:"Growth mindset",
 s:"The belief that abilities can be developed through effort, good strategies and help from others.",
 l:"A growth mindset is the view that skills and intelligence are not fixed but can improve with practice. Its opposite, a fixed mindset, is the belief that you either have a talent or you don't. People with a growth mindset may see mistakes as information rather than proof of failure. The idea became very popular in schools, but later research suggests that simple interventions have smaller effects than first hoped, and that effort alone is not enough without support and good methods.",
 x:"Instead of saying “I'm bad at drawing”, she tried the growth mindset version: “I'm not good at drawing yet.”",
 f:"The terms were made famous by the American psychologist Carol Dweck, especially through her book Mindset, published in 2006.",
 js:"Believing you can get better at things by practising.",
 jl:"When something is hard, you might think “I can't do it”. A growth mindset adds one small word: “I can't do it yet.” It means believing your brain can learn and grow, like a muscle, when you practise and ask for help.",
 jx:"Ollie couldn't ride his bike at first, but with a growth mindset he kept trying and asked Dad for tips."},
{id:"p_anchoring",cat:"psy",t:"Anchoring effect",
 s:"The tendency to rely too much on the first piece of information we hear when making a judgement.",
 l:"When people estimate a number, the first figure they encounter, the anchor, pulls their answer towards it, even if it is irrelevant. A shop that shows a high “original” price makes the sale price seem like a bargain, and the first offer in a negotiation often shapes the final deal. Anchoring affects experts as well as beginners. Knowing about it helps: considering several reference points or deciding your own limit in advance can weaken its pull.",
 x:"The first price the seller named worked as an anchor, and we ended up paying more than we planned.",
 f:"In a famous 1974 study, Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman spun a wheel of fortune before asking people a factual question about countries; the random number on the wheel influenced their estimates.",
 js:"When the first number you hear sticks in your head and changes your guess.",
 jl:"If someone asks “Are there more or fewer than one hundred sweets in the jar?”, your guess will probably be close to one hundred. That first number is like an anchor that holds a boat in place – it holds your thinking near it.",
 jx:"The toy said “was thirty, now twenty”, and Mila thought it was cheap – that's the anchoring effect."},
{id:"p_attachment",cat:"psy",t:"Attachment",
 s:"The deep emotional bond between a child and the people who care for them.",
 l:"Attachment theory says that babies and young children naturally seek closeness to trusted carers, especially when they are frightened, tired or unwell. When carers respond fairly consistently and warmly, children tend to develop secure attachment, using the carer as a safe base from which to explore. Children can be securely attached to several people, not only parents. Researchers also study how early bonds may influence later relationships, though people's attachment patterns can change through life.",
 x:"The toddler kept glancing back at his dad while exploring the playground – a sign of secure attachment.",
 f:"Attachment theory was developed by the British psychiatrist John Bowlby. The American-Canadian psychologist Mary Ainsworth created a well-known observation method called the Strange Situation.",
 js:"The strong, loving bond between a child and the people who look after them.",
 jl:"Little children feel safe when the people who love them are near. If they're scared, they run to them for a hug, and then feel brave enough to go and play again. That special bond is called attachment, and you can have it with parents, grandparents and other carers.",
 jx:"Baby Rosa crawled off to explore, but kept looking back to check Mum was still there."},
{id:"p_burnout",cat:"psy",j:false,t:"Burnout",
 s:"A state of deep exhaustion caused by long-term stress at work that has not been managed.",
 l:"Burnout develops slowly when work demands stay too high for too long without enough rest, support or control. The World Health Organization describes three main signs: feeling drained of energy, growing mentally distant or cynical about one's job, and feeling less effective. It is not the same as ordinary tiredness, and a weekend off rarely fixes it. Carers and students can experience similar exhaustion. Recovery often needs changes to the situation itself, not only to the individual.",
 x:"After two years of overtime, she recognised the signs of burnout and talked to her manager about her workload.",
 f:"The WHO's classification of diseases, ICD-11, lists burnout as an occupational phenomenon rather than a medical condition. The psychologist Herbert Freudenberger popularised the term in the 1970s."},
{id:"p_alexithymia",cat:"psy",t:"Alexithymia",
 s:"Difficulty recognising, understanding and describing one's own emotions.",
 l:"People with alexithymia may feel that something is going on inside but struggle to name it: is it anger, sadness, worry or just hunger? They may notice body sensations, like a racing heart, without linking them to feelings. Alexithymia is not a diagnosis but a trait that varies from person to person, and it is more common among autistic people, though many autistic people do not have it. Tools such as emotion wheels or body check-ins can help.",
 x:"Because of her alexithymia, she used a feelings chart to work out whether she was tired or upset.",
 f:"The term was introduced in the 1970s by the psychiatrist Peter Sifneos, from Greek words meaning “no words for emotions”.",
 js:"When it's hard to know or say what you are feeling.",
 jl:"Sometimes you feel something inside – maybe a tight tummy or hot cheeks – but you're not sure if it's being sad, cross or worried. For some people, this happens a lot. It has a long name: alexithymia. Feelings charts with faces and colours can help.",
 jx:"Noah pointed to the picture of a worried face to show his teacher how he felt."},
{id:"p_en_stiff_upper_lip",cat:"psy",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"Stiff upper lip",
 s:"The traditional British ideal of staying calm and not showing emotion in difficult times.",
 l:"Keeping a stiff upper lip means hiding fear or sadness and carrying on as if nothing were wrong; a trembling lip is a sign of being about to cry. The attitude was long praised in Britain as dignified and brave. Today many people see its limits: bottling up feelings can make problems harder to deal with, and talking about mental health is increasingly encouraged. The phrase is now often used with a touch of irony.",
 x:"Her grandfather kept a stiff upper lip at the funeral, but cried later at home.",
 f:"The poster “Keep Calm and Carry On” was printed by the British government in 1939 but hardly used. A copy found in a second-hand bookshop in Alnwick in 2000 made it famous around the world.",
 js:"Trying to stay calm and not show you're upset.",
 jl:"In Britain people say “keep a stiff upper lip” when they mean “stay calm and be brave”. But it's okay to show feelings too! Talking to someone you trust when you're sad or scared really helps.",
 jx:"Grandpa said he used to keep a stiff upper lip, but now he thinks talking about feelings is better."},
/* ---------- BUSINESS ---------- */
{id:"b_break_even",cat:"biz",t:"Break-even point",
 s:"The point at which income exactly covers costs, so there is neither profit nor loss.",
 l:"A business reaches break-even when the money it takes in equals the money it spends. Below that point it makes a loss; above it, a profit. To find it, owners compare fixed costs, such as rent, which stay the same however much they sell, with the profit made on each item after its own costs. Knowing the break-even point helps set prices, decide whether an idea is worth starting and plan how long it will take to become profitable.",
 x:"The new café needs to sell about two hundred coffees a day just to reach the break-even point.",
 f:"Break-even analysis is one of the first calculations banks ask for when someone applies for a loan to start a business.",
 js:"When you've earned back exactly what you spent.",
 jl:"Your lemonade stand cost ten pounds for lemons, sugar and cups. When you've sold enough cups to earn back those ten pounds, you've reached break-even. Every cup after that is profit!",
 jx:"At the school fair, our cake stall reached break-even by lunchtime."},
{id:"b_margin",cat:"biz",t:"Profit margin",
 s:"The share of the selling price that is left as profit after costs.",
 l:"Profit margin shows how much of every pound of sales a business keeps. Gross margin subtracts only the direct cost of making or buying the product, while net margin also takes away rent, wages, taxes and other expenses. Margins are usually shown as percentages, which makes it easier to compare businesses of different sizes. Supermarkets often work on thin margins and sell large volumes, while luxury brands sell fewer items with much higher margins.",
 x:"The shop raised its prices slightly to improve its profit margin.",
 f:"A company can have high sales and still lose money if its margins are too low to cover its overheads.",
 js:"How much money is left after paying for what something cost.",
 jl:"If you make a bracelet with beads that cost one pound and sell it for three pounds, you keep two pounds. That left-over part is your profit margin. The bigger the margin, the more you keep from each sale.",
 jx:"Ava worked out her margin: each card cost her fifty pence to make, and she sold it for one pound."},
{id:"b_outsourcing",cat:"biz",t:"Outsourcing",
 s:"Paying an outside company to do work that a business could do itself.",
 l:"Businesses outsource tasks such as cleaning, accounting, customer service or IT so they can focus on what they do best, save money or get specialist skills. The outside firm may be local or in another country; the latter is sometimes called offshoring. Outsourcing can bring flexibility, but it also has risks: less control over quality, dependence on a supplier and worries about jobs and data security.",
 x:"The company outsourced its payroll to an accounting firm.",
 f:"The opposite move, bringing work back in-house, is called insourcing.",
 js:"Asking another company to do a job for you.",
 jl:"A bakery is great at making bread, but maybe not at fixing computers. So it pays another company to look after its computers. Getting someone outside to do a job for you is called outsourcing.",
 jx:"Our school outsources lunches to a catering company."},
{id:"b_franchise",cat:"biz",t:"Franchise",
 s:"An arrangement in which a business owner pays to use another company's brand and way of working.",
 l:"In a franchise, a company called the franchisor lets independent business owners, the franchisees, run outlets under its name. Franchisees usually pay a start-up fee and an ongoing share of sales, and in return receive the brand, training, recipes or products and marketing. Many fast-food restaurants, hotels and gyms work this way. Franchisees own their business but must follow the franchisor's rules closely.",
 x:"She bought a franchise of a popular coffee chain and opened a branch in her town.",
 f:"Franchise comes from Old French franc, “free”; originally it meant a special privilege or freedom granted by a ruler. In sport, a franchise also means a team that holds a licence to play in a league.",
 js:"Running your own shop that uses a famous company's name and ideas.",
 jl:"Have you noticed that some restaurant chains look the same in every town? Often each one is run by a local owner who pays the big company to use its name, recipes and logo. That's a franchise.",
 jx:"Ben's aunt runs a franchise of an ice-cream chain near the beach."},
{id:"b_deadline",cat:"biz",t:"Deadline",
 s:"The latest time or date by which something must be done.",
 l:"A deadline is a fixed point by which a task must be finished or submitted, such as a project, an application or a tax return. Clear deadlines help teams plan and coordinate their work, but unrealistic ones can cause stress and poor quality. Breaking a big task into smaller steps with their own mini-deadlines often makes it easier to manage, especially for people with ADHD, who may find distant deadlines hard to feel until they are very close.",
 x:"The deadline for the report is Friday at noon.",
 f:"The word first meant a real line: during the American Civil War, it was a boundary in a prison camp that prisoners were forbidden to cross. Later, printers used it for the time a page had to be ready.",
 js:"The time by which something must be finished.",
 jl:"If your teacher says “hand in your project on Friday”, Friday is the deadline. After that it's too late. A good trick is to do a little bit every day so you're not rushing at the end.",
 jx:"The deadline for the poster was Thursday, so Isla did a bit each evening."},
{id:"b_en_ltd",cat:"biz",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"Ltd and plc",
 s:"The endings of UK company names that show whether a company is private (Ltd) or public (plc).",
 l:"Ltd stands for “limited”, a private limited company. Its shares cannot be offered to the general public, and its owners' liability is limited to what they have invested. Plc means public limited company: its shares can be offered to the public and may be traded on a stock exchange, and it must meet stricter rules, including a minimum amount of share capital. All UK companies are registered with Companies House, where basic information about them is publicly available.",
 x:"She set up her design studio as a Ltd company to separate her personal and business finances.",
 f:"Anyone can search the public register at Companies House to see a company's directors and filed accounts.",
 js:"Letters after a company's name that tell you what kind of company it is.",
 jl:"In Britain, company names often end with “Ltd” or “plc”. Ltd means the company is private, owned by a few people. Plc means anyone can buy a small piece of it, called a share.",
 jx:"Sam spotted “Ltd” on the side of the van and asked Dad what it meant."}
]);
